<TopicPage slug="subjects/environmental-science/advanced/environmental-economics-and-policy">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Environmental economics is <strong>the discipline of pricing what markets ignore</strong> —
    and environmental policy is its instruments in the political world. The science says "what";
    this field says "how much, who pays, and by which mechanism".
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="pricing externalities, the Stern framing, the instruments, the international problem" widget="equationStep" config={envEconCfg} />

  <h2>Debates professionals actually have</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Tax vs cap:</strong> a carbon tax fixes the price and lets quantity move; a cap fixes quantity and lets price swing. Hybrids (price collars) exist — the design details are where the politics hides.</li>
    <li><strong>Offsets, carefully:</strong> additionality (would it have happened anyway?), permanence, leakage — the forest-offset scandals (2023 investigations) are textbook failures of these three tests, not of the concept\'s arithmetic.</li>
    <li><strong>The discount-rate war:</strong> Stern\'s near-zero rate → aggressive action now; Nordhaus\'s market-based rate → gradualism. A philosophy-of-intergenerational-justice dispute (the ethics ladder) dressed as a parameter.</li>
    <li><strong>Co-benefits often decide policy:</strong> air-quality gains from coal phase-out pay for much of the transition locally (the air lesson\'s 7 million deaths) — the strongest political argument is frequently health, not climate.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Stern Review (2006); Nordhaus, The Climate Casino; World Bank carbon-pricing dashboard; Paris Agreement (2015); the economics ladder\'s externalities lesson.</p>
</TopicPage>
